class Solution {
    public int[] productExceptSelf(int[] nums) {
        int n = nums.length;
        int[] ans = new int[n];
        
        int[] prefix = new int[n];
        int[] suffix = new int[n];

        // Base cases: products with nothing next to them start at 1
        prefix[0] = 1;
        suffix[n-1] = 1;

        // 1. Calculate all Prefix products (Left to Right)
        for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
            prefix[i] = prefix[i-1] * nums[i-1];
        }

        // 2. Calculate all Suffix products (Right to Left)
        for (int i = n-2; i >= 0; i--) {
            suffix[i] = suffix[i+1] * nums[i+1];
        }

        // 3. Combine them into the final answer array
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            ans[i] = prefix[i] * suffix[i];
        }

        return ans;
    }
}

Let's do a complete, line-by-line dry run of this optimized $O(n)$ space solution! This will show you exactly how the two arrays (prefix and suffix) collect their data and how they combine to give the final result without any nested loops.
We will use our classic example: nums = [1, 2, 3, 4] (where length n = 4).


🧱 Setup Before Any Loops Start
We allocate memory for our arrays. In Java, new int[4] fills them with 0 by default.
	• nums = [1, 2, 3, 4]
	• prefix = [0, 0, 0, 0]
	• suffix = [0, 0, 0, 0]
	• ans = [0, 0, 0, 0]
Then we set up our base cases:
	• prefix[0] = 1; $\rightarrow$ Because there are no elements to the left of the first element.
	• suffix[3] = 1; $\rightarrow$ Because there are no elements to the right of the last element.
State of arrays now:
	• prefix = [1, 0, 0, 0]
	• suffix = [0, 0, 0, 1]


🔄 Loop 1: Calculating Prefix Products (Left to Right)
The loop formula is: prefix[i] = prefix[i-1] * nums[i-1];
This means: "The product of everything before me is (the product of everything before my neighbor) $\times$ (my neighbor's value)."
	• i = 1:
		○ prefix[1] = prefix[0] * nums[0]
		○ prefix[1] = 1 * 1 = 1
		○ prefix state: [1, 1, 0, 0]
	• i = 2:
		○ prefix[2] = prefix[1] * nums[1]
		○ prefix[2] = 1 * 2 = 2
		○ prefix state: [1, 1, 2, 0]
	• i = 3:
		○ prefix[3] = prefix[2] * nums[2]
		○ prefix[3] = 2 * 3 = 6
		○ prefix state: [1, 1, 2, 6]
End of Loop 1: We have successfully captured all left-side products!


🔄 Loop 2: Calculating Suffix Products (Right to Left)
The loop starts at i = n-2 (which is 4 - 2 = 1) and moves backwards.
The loop formula is: suffix[i] = suffix[i+1] * nums[i+1];
This means: "The product of everything after me is (the product of everything after my right neighbor) $\times$ (my right neighbor's value)."
	• i = 2:
		○ suffix[2] = suffix[3] * nums[3]
		○ suffix[2] = 1 * 4 = 4
		○ suffix state: [0, 0, 4, 1]
	• i = 1:
		○ suffix[1] = suffix[2] * nums[2]
		○ suffix[1] = 4 * 3 = 12
		○ suffix state: [0, 12, 4, 1]
	• i = 0:
		○ suffix[0] = suffix[1] * nums[1]
		○ suffix[0] = 12 * 2 = 24
		○ suffix state: [24, 12, 4, 1]
End of Loop 2: We have successfully captured all right-side products!


🔄 Loop 3: Combining into Final Answer (Left $\times$ Right)
Now we simply multiply the left tracking with the right tracking for each index i: ans[i] = prefix[i] * suffix[i];
	• i = 0: ans[0] = prefix[0] * suffix[0] $\rightarrow$ 1 * 24 = 24
	• i = 1: ans[1] = prefix[1] * suffix[1] $\rightarrow$ 1 * 12 = 12
	• i = 2: ans[2] = prefix[2] * suffix[2] $\rightarrow$ 2 * 4 = 8
	• i = 3: ans[3] = prefix[3] * suffix[3] $\rightarrow$ 6 * 1 = 6
Final ans Array returned: [24, 12, 8, 6]


💡 Why this is a huge win
Look at how clean the work was. We never had to recalculate anything from scratch.
	• Loop 1 did $4$ steps.
	• Loop 2 did $4$ steps.
	• Loop 3 did $4$ steps.
Total operations = $4 + 4 + 4 = 12$ steps.
Compare that to your brute force which took $4 \times 4 = 16$ steps. If $n = 100,000$, this optimized version takes only $300,000$ operations instead of $10$ billion!
